Myth: In the time of endless gray, the world knew neither joy nor despair. The people existed in a fog, unable to feel deeply or dream vividly.

A painter named Sera grew weary of the colorless world. She gathered all the pigments she could find—ochre from clay, blue from stones, red from berries—but when she tried to paint, the colors turned gray on her canvas.

In frustration, she threw her brush at the sky. It stuck there, suspended in the mist.

"Why won't you accept color?" she cried.

The fog answered, "I have forgotten how."

Sera understood then. She began to paint not things, but feelings. She painted her loneliness in deep violet, her curiosity in bright yellow, her love in warm rose. Each emotion she painted, she spoke aloud, teaching the fog to remember.

As she worked, the fog began to absorb her colors. Slowly, the gray separated—blue descended to become the sea, green spread across the land, white rose to form clouds.

The sky itself became her canvas, ever-changing with the colors of emotion.

Now when people feel deeply, they look up and see their feelings reflected in sunsets, storms, and rainbows—Sera's gift of a world that remembers how to feel.
